  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Each dimension is given to the nearest $1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.5\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *smallest* possible area of the container. Therefore, we require the *lower bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe lower bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=11.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 3\\,\\text{m}-0.5\\,\\text{m}=2.5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible area of the container is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =11.5\\,\\text{m}\\times 2.5\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =28.75\\,{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & ≈29\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest square meter.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
